### What rule do you want to change?

array-callback-return

### What change do you want to make?

Generate more warnings

### How do you think the change should be implemented?

A new option

### Example code

```js
/* eslint array-callback-return: "error" */

const results = Array.fromAsync(
    values,
    value => { not_a_property: value }
);
```

[**Playground link**](https://eslint.org/play/#)

### What does the rule currently do for this code?

No problem is currently reported.

### What will the rule do after it's changed?

It will report a problem when the callback passed to `Array.fromAsync` does not return a value, consistent with the behavior for `Array.from`.

In addition, because `Array.fromAsync` commonly uses async callbacks, it may be useful for the rule to also flag async functions that do not return a value. At the moment, async callbacks are ignored, but for `Array.fromAsync` this is likely the more common case.

```js
// This is an error:
const a = Array.from(foo, bar => { /* no return */ });

// This should be also an error:
const b = Array.fromAsync(foo, bar => { /* no return */ });

// This is not an error:
const c = Array.from(foo, async bar => { /* no return */ });

// But maybe this should be an error?:
const d = Array.fromAsyc(foo, async bar => { /* no return */ });
```

[**Playground link**](https://eslint.org/play/#)

### Participation

- [x] I am willing to submit a pull request to implement this change.

### Additional comments

[`Array.fromAsync`](https://github.com/tc39/proposal-array-from-async#readme) has reached TC39 stage 4, so we should support it in core rules. See also #20437.

### What rule do you want to change?

no-invalid-this

### What change do you want to make?

Generate fewer warnings

### How do you think the change should be implemented?

A new default behavior

### Example code

```js
/* eslint no-invalid-this: "error" */

const contents = await Array.fromAsync(
    ["file1.txt", "file2.txt"],
    async function (file) {
        return await this.read(file);
    },
    { read: file => readFile(file, 'utf8') }
);
```

[**Playground link**](https://eslint.org/play/#
)

### What does the rule currently do for this code?

A problem is reported:

```text
  6:22  error  Unexpected 'this'  no-invalid-this
```

### What will the rule do after it's changed?

No problem will be reported.

### Participation

- [x] I am willing to submit a pull request to implement this change.

### Additional comments

[`Array.fromAsync`](https://github.com/tc39/proposal-array-from-async#readme) has reached TC39 stage 4 in ES2026. It follows the same signature and similar semantics as `Array.from`, so I think the rule should treat it in the same way when handling `this` in the callback function. See also #20438.

### Environment

Node version: v24.12.0
npm version: v11.6.2
Local ESLint version: v10.0.0-beta.0 (Currently used)
Global ESLint version: v1.0.0
Operating System: darwin 25.1.0


### What parser are you using?

Default (Espree)

### What did you do?

```js
/*eslint no-redeclare: "error"*/

try {
    undefined();
} catch (a) {
    var a = 10;
}

console.log(a);
```


### What did you expect to happen?

The rule should report the redeclaration of `a` inside the catch block.

### What actually happened?

No problem is being reported.

