### Participation

- [x] I am willing to submit a pull request for this issue.

### Additional comments

I'm not entirely sure about this, so please double-check.

A `var` declaration inside a catch block that uses the same name as the catch parameter actually redeclares the same variable in the current scope rather than creating a new one. Because of that, it seems to me that the `no-redeclare` rule should report this case.

This is different from a `var` declaration inside a catch block with a different name than the catch parameter, which does declare a new variable in the outer scope, e.g.:

```js

try {
    undefined();
} catch (a) {
    var b = 10;
}

console.log(b);
```
